course: books
lesson: 56
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IX, part 1: Wayfarers All (change in the air)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Summer is still warm and golden, but the Rat feels restless and doesn't know why: the cuckoo is long silent, many birds are gone, and at night he seems to hear wings beating overhead, all heading south.
explain: The author says the countryside is like a big hotel at the end of the holiday season: the guests pack, pay and leave one by one, and those who stay on feel unsettled and a little cross — why can't everyone just stay and be jolly?
explain: The Rat wanders into a field of tall wheat to visit his small friends there, but the field-mice and harvest-mice are all busy digging, packing trunks and studying plans of new little flats for the winter.
explain: They tell him to stop standing about idle and help; he invites them for a row or a picnic, and they say «perhaps some other day»; he trips over a hat-box, falls, and is told off for not looking where he is going.
explain: Grumpy and a bit lonely, the Rat goes back to his faithful old river, which never packs up, moves house or goes away for the winter.
fact: The chapter's name is «Wayfarers All»: wayfarers are travellers on foot, people on their way somewhere.
fact: It is the end of summer: «green had given way to gold» in the fields, and «rowans were reddening» (a rowan is a tree with bright red berries).
fact: But «there was a feeling in the air of change and departure»: «The cuckoo, of course, had long been silent», and the robin was «beginning to assert himself once more» (robins sing again in autumn).
fact: The Rat sees that the birds' flights are «taking daily a southing tendency» (moving more and more southwards); at night he hears «the beat and quiver of impatient pinions» (pinions = wings): birds migrating.
fact: «Nature's Grand Hotel has its Season, like the others»: the departing birds are like hotel guests who «pack, pay, and depart»; the ones «staying on, en pension» (living there with meals included) feel «unsettled, depressed, and inclined to be querulous» (complaining).
fact: The stay-at-homes ask: «Why this craving for change? Why not stay on quietly here, like us, and be jolly?»
fact: «The Rat was a self-sufficing sort of animal, rooted to the land, and, whoever went, he stayed»; still he felt «some of its influence in his bones».
fact: He wanders into «the great sea of wheat», a «forest of stiff strong stalks that carried their own golden sky away over his head».
fact: The field-mice and harvest-mice are digging, studying «plans and drawings of small flats ... situated conveniently near the Stores» (the shop), and hauling out «dusty trunks and dress-baskets».
fact: Piles of «wheat, oats, barley, beech-mast and nuts» lie ready to be moved (beech-mast = the little nuts of the beech tree).
fact: They cry: «Here's old Ratty! Come and bear a hand, Rat, and don't stand about idle!» (bear a hand = help).
fact: The Rat says: «You know it isn't time to be thinking of winter quarters yet, by a long way!» (winter quarters = a home for the winter).
fact: A field-mouse explains they must move «before those horrid machines begin clicking round the fields» (the harvest machines), and because «the best flats get picked up so quickly nowadays».
fact: The Rat suggests a row, a stroll or a picnic; the field-mouse answers: «Well, I think not to-day, thank you ... Perhaps some other day — when we've more time».
fact: The Rat trips «over a hat-box, and fell, with undignified remarks»; a field-mouse says: «If people would be more careful ... and look where they're going» and «Mind that hold-all, Rat!» (a hold-all is a big soft travel bag).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «salty» = annoyed; «hun» = dear; «yinz» (Pittsburgh) = you lot; «ginnel» (North England) = a narrow alley; «bostin'» (Black Country) = great.
exercise: What season is it at the start of chapter IX? | The end of summer | Early spring | Deep midwinter | The first day of May
exercise: How does the Rat feel at the start of the chapter? | Restless, without knowing why | Happy and quite settled | Ill with a bad cold | Angry with Toad and the Mole about the boat
exercise: Which bird «had long been silent»? | The cuckoo | The robin | The owl | The willow-wren
exercise: Which bird is «beginning to assert himself once more»? | The robin | The cuckoo | The swallow | The nightingale
exercise: What is a rowan? | A tree with bright red berries | A small boat that is rowed | A kind of field-mouse | A little brown bird that sings all night long
exercise: Which way are the birds' flights moving more and more each day? | South | North | Up into the hills | Into the Wild Wood
exercise: What does the Rat think he hears at night, lying in bed? | Wings of birds beating overhead in the dark | Toad's motor-car on the far high road | The weir roaring after heavy rain | The field-mice singing carols outside his window, as at Christmas
exercise: What does «pinions» mean? | Wings | Pine trees | Small pins | Onions
exercise: The author compares the countryside to what? | A grand hotel at the end of its season | A railway station on a busy morning | A school at the start of term | A grim old castle with a deep dungeon
exercise: In the hotel picture, what do the departing birds do? | Pack, pay and depart, like guests | Stay on until next summer, like boarders | Cook the meals for the other guests | Sweep the carpets and close the rooms
exercise: How do those who stay on feel? | Unsettled and inclined to grumble | Glad that everyone has gone at last | Frightened that the hotel will close | Sleepy and ready for bed
exercise: What does «self-sufficing» mean, said of the Rat? | Needing nothing more than his own place and life | Needing lots of friends around him every hour | Wanting to travel all the time to new places | Unable to look after himself at all without help from others
exercise: Where does the Rat go to visit his small friends? | Into a great field of wheat | Into the Wild Wood | Up to Toad Hall | Down to the island by the weir
exercise: How do the wheat stalks look to the small Rat? | Like a forest with a golden sky over his head | Like a dull grey wall that blocks his way | Like the black trees of the Wild Wood in snow | Like a river of green water flowing past
exercise: What are the field-mice doing? | Digging, packing and looking at plans of flats | Having a big picnic in the sun | Singing carols in a semicircle | Sleeping snugly in their winter beds already, though it is still warm
exercise: What are the flats «conveniently near»? | The Stores | The river | The castle | The station
exercise: Which of these is NOT in the piles ready to be moved? | Buttered toast | Wheat and oats | Barley | Beech-mast and nuts
exercise: What do the field-mice ask the Rat to do? | Bear a hand and not stand about idle | Sing them a song while they work | Lend them his boat to carry the furniture | Go home and leave them in peace
exercise: Why are the field-mice moving so early? | Before the harvest machines come, and before the best flats go | Because the Rat has asked them to leave his field | Because the river is going to flood the wheat tonight | Because Toad has bought the whole field to make a track for his motor-cars
exercise: What does the Rat invite them to do instead? | A row, a stroll or a picnic | A trip to Toad Hall for tea | A swim in the river | A walk in the Wild Wood
exercise: What does the field-mouse answer? | «Perhaps some other day — when we've more time» | «Yes, at once! We love a picnic» | «Only if Badger comes too» | «Not ever, Rat; we don't like boats or picnics»
exercise: What does the Rat trip over? | A hat-box | A door-scraper | A rope | A teapot
exercise: What is a «hold-all»? | A big soft travel bag | A strong rope for boats | A kind of hat-box | A cupboard for food
exercise: Where does the Rat go when he leaves the field? | Back to his river | Up to the Downs | Off to Mole End | Into the town by the castle
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say about «the Wide World» beyond the Wild Wood? | «I've never been there, and I'm never going» | «I've been there often, and it's wonderful» | «I'm going there next summer with Toad» | «It's full of weasels and stoats and nothing else»
exercise: Looking back: in chapter V, what were the field-mice doing when they came to Mole End? | Singing carols in a semicircle with a lantern | Packing trunks to move into new flats | Selling sardines and biscuits from a basket | Hunting all over the fore-court for the Mole's lost front-door key
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, how did the Rat answer Toad's invitation to travel by caravan at first? | «I'm not coming, and that's flat» | «Yes! I've always longed to go» | «Only if the Mole drives the horse» | «Let me pack my boat first»
exercise: ★★★ In the hotel picture, who are the guests who leave and who are the boarders who stay? | Birds that fly south leave; animals like the Rat stay | The Rat leaves; the birds stay all winter | The field-mice leave; the swallows stay | Toad leaves for the seaside; Badger and the Mole stay at home
exercise: ★★★ Count: how many kinds of food lie in the field-mice's piles (wheat, oats, barley, beech-mast and nuts)? | Five | Four | Three | Six
test: «Ratty's salty cos the mice won't come for a picnic.» (salty = annoyed) Is that fair? | Yes: they're too busy packing, and he grumbles and stomps off | No: they come at once and have a lovely picnic | No: the Rat never even asks them | Yes: the mice throw a hat-box at him on purpose and laugh
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | Everyone around the Rat seems to be getting ready to leave | It is deep winter and snow is falling | The Rat is packing his own trunk and hat-box for the long journey South | The cuckoo is singing loudly in the hedge
test: «It's only the birds packing up.» Put it right. | The field-mice and harvest-mice are packing up too | Only the cuckoo is packing up, nobody else | Nobody is packing; it's a normal summer day | Only Toad is packing, for a holiday at the seaside
test: Why does the Rat say «You won't be 'free' as you call it much this side of Christmas»? | He grumbles that they'll be busy moving for months | He thinks they will be in prison like Toad | He means Christmas is coming next week | He has bought them all Christmas presents and wants to hand them out
test: What would a field-mouse probably say if the Rat came back next week? | Still too busy, perhaps another day | Come in, we have finished everything | Go away, this is our field | Help us carry Toad's motor-car
test: Why is the river called «faithful, steady-going»? | It never packs up or goes away for the winter | It runs very slowly and never floods | It belongs to the Rat, who bought it | It carries the Rat's boat for free wherever he likes
test: Mia, a bit sad: «the Rat's not bothered by any of it, hun.» (hun = dear) Put it right. | He stays, but he feels the change «in his bones» | He is not bothered at all, ever | He is so bothered that he leaves at once | He is bothered only by Toad and his motor-cars
test: Joe from Pittsburgh: «yinz see, the mice are moving cos the Rat told them to.» (yinz = you lot) Put it right. | They move before the harvest machines come, and to get good flats | The Rat ordered them out of his field | The weasels chased them out | Badger asked them all to come and live in his tunnels for the winter
test: What does the field-mouse's stiff remark «If people would be more careful» show? | The busy mice are short-tempered and won't stop for him | The mice are very worried about the Rat's leg | The mice are glad he fell and laugh | The mice don't know who the Rat is and think he's a stranger
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat call the river? | «It's my world, and I don't want any other» | «A dull fusty old river» | «A nuisance in February» | «The long road to the sea and the South»
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what did the Rat do a great deal in winter? | Slept, rising late, and scribbled poetry | Went skating on the frozen river | Travelled south with the swallows | Drove motor-cars up and down the lanes with Toad
test: Looking back: in chapter VII the Rat heard a call in the music. Who feels a pull to go somewhere in this part? | The birds, the field-mice, and a little the Rat himself | Only Toad, who wants a motor-car | Only Otter, who wants Portly back | Nobody at all, not even the birds
test: Looking back: at Mole End in chapter V, the Mole felt glad to have a home to come back to. What is the Rat's «home» that never moves? | His river | His boat | Toad Hall | Badger's house
test: Looking back: who had to be dragged out of his bunk by force in chapter II, because he no longer liked the simple life? | Toad | The Mole | The Rat | The old grey horse
test: ★★★ Careful: «whoever went, he stayed». Who is «he»? | The Rat | The cuckoo | The robin | A field-mouse
test: ★★★ The field-mice say «Of course, we're early, we know that». Why do they still start? | Waiting risks the machines and losing the best flats | They have nothing else to do in summer | The Rat told them to start early | They simply like packing much more than picnics
exam: Identify the season in which the events of the ninth chapter commence. | The close of summer, the fields having turned from green to gold | The depth of winter, the river being frozen from bank to bank | The beginning of spring, with the cuckoo newly arrived | Midsummer's night, the moon being at the full over the water-meadows
exam: State the comparison employed by the author in respect of the departure of the birds. | The closing of a grand hotel at the end of its season | The departure of a train from a crowded station | The breaking-up of a school at the end of term | The dispersal of an army after a long and arduous battle
exam: Ascertain the reason furnished by the field-mice for their early removal. | The impending arrival of the harvesting machines and the scarcity of desirable flats | An order received from the Water Rat to vacate the field forthwith | The flooding of the river and the destruction of their burrows | The desire to accompany the swallows on their voyage southward
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Water Rat's conduct in the field? | He declined to assist, tripped over a hat-box and withdrew to his river | He assisted the field-mice with great industry until nightfall | He accepted their invitation to inspect the new flats | He persuaded the field-mice to abandon their preparations and join him upon the river
